// Concept texts, batch 9 (names POST-TRAUMATIC to SEASONAL AFFECTIVE DISORDER).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["POST-TRAUMATIC", "D", "A state that follows a traumatic event, when its effects keep showing up in daily life.", "Memories, reactions and moods may keep returning long after the event. The body can stay on alert.", "Jumpiness, avoiding reminders, sudden mood shifts, or trouble sleeping after a frightening event.", "Strong reactions after a terrible event are a common human response. They are not a sign of weakness.", ""],
  ["POST-TRAUMATIC GROWTH", "U", "Positive personal change that can follow a painful event, such as deeper values or closer bonds.", "Growth can come alongside pain, not in place of it. It may bring a fuller appreciation for life.", "Valuing relationships more, setting clearer priorities, or feeling more compassion for others.", "Growth does not mean the event was worth it or that suffering is required. It can follow the hard work of healing.", ""],
  ["POST-TRAUMATIC INTEGRATION", "K", "Gradually fitting a traumatic experience into your life story, so it no longer controls your days.", "The memory may still hurt, but it can feel less sharp and less all-consuming. Pieces may come together slowly.", "Talking about the past calmly, telling the story in a clear order, or setting limits on its pull.", "Integration does not mean forgetting or forgiving the harm. It means the event becomes one part of a larger life.", ""],
  ["POST-TRAUMATIC MEANING", "U", "Finding a sense of meaning or purpose after living through a traumatic experience.", "Meaning can bring a steadier feeling and a sense that pain was not wasted. It may also feel complicated.", "Helping others who face similar events, speaking up for change, or choosing new values.", "Meaning is not a duty to turn pain into something useful. It should be your own, at your own pace.", ""],
  ["PRADER-WILLI SYNDROME", "B", "A rare genetic condition that can cause constant hunger, learning differences and behavior challenges.", "Hunger may feel intense and hard to control, and daily routines can feel tough to keep. Stress can rise around food.", "Searching for food, strong reactions to changes in routine, or needing close support with meals.", "It is a medical condition, not a lack of self-control. Steady routines, medical care and support can make a real difference.", ""],
  ["PREMATURE FORGIVENESS", "T", "Forgiving someone too quickly, before the harm is acknowledged or the hurt has had time to heal.", "It can bring a quick sense of peace or a wish to end conflict. Anger or sadness may stay unspoken underneath.", "Saying 'it is fine' right away, or dropping a complaint before any change has happened.", "Forgiveness is healthy when it comes from real understanding. Forgiving too early can leave the harm open to repeat.", ""],
  ["PREMENSTRUAL DYSPHORIC DISORDER", "D", "A severe mood condition that flares in the days before a period and usually eases once it starts.", "Mood can drop sharply, with anger, hopelessness or anxiety. Everything can feel harder and more overwhelming.", "Irritability, tearfulness, withdrawing, or conflict with others just before a period.", "It is a real medical condition, not a character flaw or an overreaction. Treatment and a doctor's care can help.", ""],
  ["PRESSURED", "A", "Feeling pushed to hurry, or speech that rushes on and is hard to interrupt.", "Tension can build fast, and the mind may rush toward the nearest answer. Clear thinking can feel out of reach.", "Hurrying speech, quick decisions, or visible tension when deadlines and demands pile up.", "Feeling pressured is common with deadlines and demands. A pause, one clear priority and a realistic plan can help.", ""],
  ["PRIDE", "M", "A warm sense of satisfaction in your own effort, or in the achievements of people you love.", "Pride can lift mood and confidence. Too much of it can turn into a need to look superior.", "Sharing achievements, standing tall, or speaking with quiet satisfaction about results.", "Pride is not always arrogance. Healthy pride rests on real effort and stays open to other people.", ""],
  ["PROFOUND GRATITUDE", "U", "A deep, lasting thankfulness for the people and kindnesses that have shaped your life.", "Gratitude can feel warm and humble. It can ease loneliness and loosen the grip of resentment.", "Thanking people sincerely, pausing to notice small gifts, or speaking about others with warmth.", "Gratitude does not require ignoring pain or thanking people for harm. It can sit alongside grief and anger.", ""],
  ["PROFOUND INNER WORTHINESS", "U", "A deep, settled belief that you deserve love and respect, simply as you are.", "Worthiness can feel calm and secure, even after mistakes. It softens the urge to earn approval all the time.", "Accepting kind words, setting healthy limits, and treating yourself with respect.", "Inner worthiness does not depend on achievements or other people's approval. It does not mean you never doubt yourself.", ""],
  ["PROFOUND LIFE GRATITUDE", "U", "Deep appreciation for life itself, including its hard moments as well as its beautiful ones.", "Life can feel precious and worth the effort. Even difficult days may hold something to be thankful for.", "Speaking warmly about life, savoring simple moments, or helping others feel grateful too.", "Gratitude for life does not mean feeling happy every day. It can grow alongside sadness and loss.", ""],
  ["PROFOUND SELF-FORGIVENESS", "U", "Letting go of harsh self-blame for past mistakes, while still taking honest responsibility for them.", "Self-forgiveness can bring relief and room to grow. It often comes slowly, after honest reflection.", "Owning a mistake clearly, making amends where possible, and moving on without self-punishment.", "Self-forgiveness does not erase harm or excuse it. It is the choice to stop punishing yourself and to learn.", ""],
  ["PROFOUND SELF-RESPECT", "U", "A deep respect for yourself that shapes how you allow others to treat you.", "Self-respect can feel steady and dignified. It makes it easier to say no to what harms you.", "Keeping commitments, speaking up for yourself, and walking away from mistreatment.", "Self-respect is not pride or arrogance. It is recognizing your own worth and acting in line with it.", ""],
  ["PROGRESSIVE MUSCLE RELAXATION", "K", "A relaxation exercise in which you tense and then release each group of muscles in turn.", "Releasing tension can bring warmth, heaviness and calm. The body may feel looser and sleepier.", "Sitting or lying quietly, breathing slowly, and tightening then relaxing the hands, arms, legs and face.", "It is a skill that improves with practice, not a quick fix. Skip any tension that causes pain, cramp or injury.", ""],
  ["PROJECTION", "T", "Seeing your own unwanted feelings or traits in other people, instead of noticing them in yourself.", "The feeling can stay out of awareness, so others seem to be the source of the problem. Relief may follow for a while.", "Accusing others of what you are doing, such as calling someone angry while you are the angry one.", "Everyone projects sometimes. It becomes a problem when it blocks honest self-reflection and causes unfair conflict.", ""],
  ["PROJECTIONIST", "R", "A person who often pins their own feelings or flaws onto others and treats them as the cause.", "The target may feel unfairly accused and confused about what is really happening. Trust can wear down.", "Regularly accusing others of what they seem to be doing, and not seeing their own part.", "The habit does not make someone a villain. It can change with honesty, reflection and support.", ""],
  ["PROLONGED GRIEF DISORDER", "D", "Grief that stays very intense long after a loss and keeps daily life from moving on.", "Longing and pain can stay sharp, and the world may feel empty. Hope and interest may seem far away.", "Constant longing, withdrawing from life, or struggling with ordinary tasks many months after the loss.", "Strong grief is a natural response to loss, but this pattern is more intense and lasting than usual. It can respond to grief-focused therapy.", ""],
  ["PROPHETIC", "T", "A strong feeling that you can foresee events or have been chosen to reveal hidden truths.", "Such feelings can bring excitement, purpose or importance. They can also bring fear that the future is already set.", "Talking about foreseeing events, claiming special messages, or acting on predictions with certainty.", "Intuition is normal, and most hunches are just hunches. A fixed certainty about the future deserves a check with a doctor or trusted person.", ""],
  ["PROSOPAGNOSIA", "B", "A condition, often called face blindness, that makes recognizing faces hard, even familiar ones.", "Faces can look blank or confusing, and meeting people again can feel awkward or stressful. Names and context can be a lifeline in those moments.", "Hesitating before greeting people, relying on clothing or hairstyles, or seeming not to recognize friends.", "It is not rudeness or lack of interest. It is a brain-based difference, and people can learn other clues.", ""],
  ["PROTEUS SYNDROME", "B", "A rare genetic condition that can cause uneven overgrowth of some body parts, often with skin changes.", "Growth can be unpredictable and may bring pain, discomfort or worry about treatment. Daily life may need steady planning and medical follow-up.", "Frequent medical visits and tests, and sometimes a need for special care or equipment.", "It is not caused by anything a person did. Specialist medical care can help manage symptoms and related risks.", ""],
  ["PROUD", "M", "Feeling glad and warm about your own achievement, or about someone you care for.", "Pride can give a lift in mood and a sense of meaning. It is often simple and quiet, like a warm glow.", "Smiling when someone is praised, sharing good news, or saying 'I am proud of you'.", "Being proud of someone does not mean you own their success. It is a warm response to their effort.", ""],
  ["PSYCHOLOGICAL FACTORS AFFECTING OTHER MEDICAL CONDITIONS", "D", "Emotional stress that makes a physical illness worse, slower to heal or harder to manage.", "Stress can show up in the body as pain, fatigue or flare-ups. Worry about the illness can feel constant.", "Skipping treatment during stressful times, or symptoms rising during hard periods.", "It does not mean the illness is imaginary. Caring for both the body and the mind helps.", ""],
  ["PSYCHOPATH", "R", "A pattern marked by little empathy or guilt, and a readiness to use or harm others for gain.", "The person may feel little anxiety or remorse, and emotions can seem shallow. Others may feel confused or used.", "Charm at first, then lying, exploiting others, and showing little regret after harm.", "Not everyone who is cold or blunt has this pattern, and the word is not a casual insult. Anyone harmed by such behavior deserves protection and support.", ""],
  ["PSYCHOSIS", "D", "A state in which a person may lose touch with shared reality, with false beliefs or perceptions.", "Things may seem real that are not, and the person may feel frightened or watched. Trust in one's own mind can feel shaky.", "Speaking about things others cannot see or hear, unusual fears, or sudden changes in behavior.", "Psychosis can occur in several conditions and is treatable. It is never a moral failing, and early care helps many people recover.", "Get medical help soon. For support now, call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["PUBLIC HUMILIATION", "C", "Deliberately shaming someone in front of others to control them or lower their standing.", "The target may feel exposed, small and afraid of being mocked again. Shame can keep them quiet.", "Mocking someone in a group, posting embarrassing details online, or belittling them before others.", "Honest criticism in private is different. Public shaming is a tactic that damages trust and self-worth.", ""],
  ["PUNISHER", "R", "A person who often punishes others for mistakes, sometimes with anger, silence or withdrawal.", "Others may feel afraid of making mistakes and walk on eggshells. The punisher may feel justified in the moment.", "Harsh consequences out of proportion to the problem, long silences, or taking away things as revenge.", "Setting consequences is normal. Punishment becomes harmful when it hurts or controls instead of teaching.", ""],
  ["PUPIL CONSTRICTION (MIOSIS)", "B", "Narrowing of the pupil, the dark center of the eye, from bright light, sleep or some medicines.", "Most people do not notice it, though bright light can feel harsh. The eyes adjust over a short time.", "Small pupils in bright light, or in some people who take certain medicines.", "Small pupils are not a sign of calm or honesty. Pinpoint pupils with slow breathing or no response need emergency care right away.", ""],
  ["PUPIL DILATION (MYDRIASIS)", "B", "Widening of the pupil, the dark center of the eye, in dim light, fear, excitement or interest.", "Eyes may feel more sensitive to light. Larger pupils can come with alertness or strong emotion.", "Large, dark pupils in dim rooms, or when a person is excited, afraid or interested.", "Dilated pupils are a normal reflex, not proof of a lie or of any single feeling. Sudden change with medicine or drugs needs a doctor's review.", ""],
  ["PUPPETMASTER SHADOW", "C", "A hidden part of a person that secretly wants to steer other people's choices from behind the scenes.", "The urge to control can feel safe or clever. It can also leave a person anxious about being found out.", "Hidden agendas, arranging events behind the scenes, and quietly pushing others toward a chosen result.", "Everyone has shadow parts. Noticing them is the first step, and the pattern matters when it harms others.", ""],
  ["PURPOSE RECONNECTION", "K", "Rebuilding a sense of purpose after losing it, by returning to what matters most to you.", "Reconnecting can bring hope, though it may feel uncertain at first. Small steps can feel meaningful again.", "Writing about personal values, volunteering again, or returning to a long-neglected interest.", "Purpose does not have to be grand or permanent. It can be found in ordinary tasks and shared again over time.", ""],
  ["PURPOSEFUL DIRECTION", "U", "A clear sense of where you are heading, which guides your daily choices.", "Direction can feel steadying, with less drift and more confidence. Choices feel easier when they match your path.", "Setting clear priorities, saying no to distractions, and taking steady steps forward.", "A clear direction can change as you learn. It is not a fixed plan you must follow forever.", ""],
  ["PUSH-PULL", "R", "A relationship pattern of moving closer and then pulling away, which creates confusion and ups and downs.", "One moment feels warm and close, and the next feels cold or distant. It can leave a person anxious and unsure where they stand.", "Warm messages followed by silence, or affection followed by sudden distance.", "Everyone needs space at times. The pattern is when closeness and distance keep swinging without explanation.", ""],
  ["PUSH-PULL SHADOW", "R", "The hidden side of a push-pull pattern, where a person longs for closeness but feels threatened by it.", "Longing and fear can both be present, which can feel confusing from the inside. Closeness may start to feel unsafe.", "Reaching out, then withdrawing when someone responds warmly, often without saying why.", "Wanting closeness and fearing it at the same time is common. The pattern can ease with honest talk and support.", ""],
  ["PYGMALIONISM", "F", "A sexual interest in statues, dolls or figures that look like people.", "The interest can feel private and confusing, and may bring shame or worry about what it means. Many people feel alone with it, which can make the worry grow.", "Usually private, shown in private searches or in collections of figures.", "An interest is not the same as acting on it. Any act involving a real person without clear consent causes serious harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["PYROMANIA", "D", "A repeated urge to start fires, with tension before the act and relief afterward.", "Tension can build until a fire is set, followed by relief and often guilt or regret. The urge can return again.", "Fascination with fire, keeping fire-starting materials nearby, or repeated small fires.", "Fires can kill people and destroy homes. The urge is treatable with professional help.", ""],
  ["PYROPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of fire, flames or burning things, even when the real risk is small.", "Seeing a candle or flame can bring a rush of fear. A racing heart and an urge to leave often follow.", "Avoiding cooking, campfires or candles, and freezing or stepping away when fire appears.", "It is a real and treatable fear. Gradual exposure with a trained therapist helps many people.", ""],
  ["QUIET INNER STRENGTH", "U", "A calm, steady strength that works without needing attention or applause.", "This strength can feel grounded and private. It often shows up when things are hardest and nobody notices.", "Staying steady under pressure, listening well, and carrying on without drama.", "Quiet strength is not the same as never asking for help. Strong people can rest, cry and ask for support too.", ""],
  ["RACING", "A", "Thoughts that speed up and jump quickly from one idea to the next, hard to slow down.", "The mind can feel crowded with many ideas at once. Sleep and focus may feel out of reach.", "Talking fast, changing topics quickly, or seeming restless and unable to settle.", "Busy thoughts are common under stress. Constant racing thoughts are worth raising with a doctor.", ""],
  ["RADIANT", "U", "A warm, bright quality of joy and aliveness that shows in how you meet the day.", "Life can feel lighter and more open. Warmth may spread to the people around you.", "Smiling easily, sharing kindness, and bringing energy to ordinary moments.", "Radiance does not mean always looking cheerful. It can sit alongside sadness, fear and tears.", ""],
  ["RADIANT AUTHENTICITY", "U", "Living honestly as yourself, with an open and warm presence.", "Being real can feel relieving and bright. It can also feel exposing when others may not approve.", "Speaking plainly, admitting mistakes, and choosing values over pleasing others.", "Authenticity is not an excuse to be unkind. It means being honest and still caring about others.", ""],
  ["RADIANT BODY HONOR", "U", "Respecting your body with care and gratitude, instead of judging it against others.", "Caring for the body can feel calm and grounded. It can bring a sense of being at home in yourself.", "Resting when tired, moving in ways you enjoy, and speaking about your body with respect.", "Honoring your body is not about how it looks to others. It is about caring for its needs and limits.", ""],
  ["RADIANT CREATIVE FLOW", "U", "A bright state of creative absorption, where time seems to melt away.", "Making can feel joyful and effortless, with doubt quieting down. Afterward, a sense of satisfaction often lingers.", "Long, absorbed sessions of drawing, writing, music or building things with care.", "Flow is not something you can force. It often comes after steady practice and will not appear every day.", ""],
  ["RADIANT INNER LIGHT", "U", "A steady sense of warmth and hope that comes from within you.", "The warmth can feel calm and reliable, even when outside life is difficult. It can steady a hard day.", "Kindness in small acts, gentle humor, and a hopeful outlook during ordinary moments.", "Inner light does not mean never feeling dark. It is a source of warmth that can return after dark times.", ""],
  ["RADIANT PRESENCE", "U", "Being fully here, warm and attentive with the people and moments around you.", "Presence can feel settled and connected. Worries about the past or future may fade for a while.", "Listening closely, making eye contact, and giving full attention to the person in front of you.", "Presence is not constant intensity. It can be quiet, brief and still meaningful.", ""],
  ["RADIANT RESILIENCE", "U", "Recovering from hardship with warmth and a renewed sense of hope.", "Recovery can feel slow, then steady. Each setback you survive can add a quiet pride.", "Returning to routines after a setback, asking for support, and staying kind while still struggling.", "Resilience does not mean hiding pain or never falling apart. It includes rest, grief and help from others.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SELF-ACCEPTANCE", "U", "Accepting yourself warmly, including the parts you wish were different.", "Acceptance can feel softer than self-criticism. It can make room for change without shame.", "Speaking to yourself kindly, admitting limits without harsh judgment, and taking good care of yourself.", "Self-acceptance is not giving up on growth. You can accept yourself and still want to change.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SELF-COMPASSION", "U", "Treating yourself with warm kindness when you struggle or make mistakes.", "Compassion can soften the sting of failure. It can make a hard moment feel less lonely.", "A gentle tone with yourself, patient pauses, and practical care when you are tired or upset.", "Self-compassion is not self-indulgence. It gives you the steadiness needed to face problems honestly.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SELF-CONFIDENCE", "U", "Trusting yourself with a steady sense of your own worth and ability.", "Confidence can feel calm and dependable. It makes it easier to try new things and speak up.", "Making decisions, accepting feedback without collapsing, and standing by your choices.", "Confidence is not arrogance. Quiet confidence is still confidence, and it does not need an audience.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SELF-HONOR", "U", "Holding yourself with dignity, keeping promises to yourself and valuing your own needs.", "Self-honor can feel grounded and proud. It helps you protect your time and energy.", "Following through on your own plans, resting when needed, and refusing to accept mistreatment.", "Self-honor is not perfection. It includes forgiving yourself when you slip and starting again.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SELF-WORTH", "U", "A warm, steady belief that you matter and deserve respect.", "Self-worth can feel steady, even on hard days. It can make it easier to ask for what you need.", "Speaking up respectfully, setting limits, and treating yourself with care.", "Self-worth does not depend on success or on other people's praise. It is not a contest against others.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SOUL CONFIDENCE", "U", "A deep, warm trust in your inner direction and your values.", "Trust in your inner compass can feel calm and clear. Decisions may feel more settled.", "Acting in line with your values, speaking honestly, and returning to your path after doubt.", "Soul confidence is not the same as certainty. It can grow alongside questions and doubt.", ""],
  ["RADIANT SOUL EXPANSION", "U", "Growing inwardly, with curiosity and openness to new meaning and connection.", "Growth can feel exciting and tender. Life may seem to open up in new directions.", "Trying new ideas, reflecting on what matters, and reaching toward others with interest.", "Expansion does not have to be dramatic. Small, steady changes count as growth too.", ""],
  ["RADICAL ACCEPTANCE", "K", "Fully accepting a painful reality as it is, without approving of it, so you can respond wisely.", "Acceptance can bring a sense of relief after a long struggle against what cannot be changed. Grief may come with it.", "Pausing to name what is true now, and choosing the next helpful step instead of arguing with reality.", "Accepting reality is not agreeing with harm. It makes room for change to start from where you are.", ""],
  ["RAGE BAITING", "C", "Deliberately provoking anger in others, often online, to get attention, clicks or control.", "The target may feel angry, drained and manipulated. They may feel pushed to react in public.", "Posting bait meant to upset, mocking someone until they lash out, or celebrating their reaction.", "Strong opinions are not always rage baiting. The pattern is when provocation is the goal and anger is the prize.", ""],
  ["RAGER", "R", "A person who often explodes in anger, with outbursts that can frighten or hurt others.", "Anger can rise fast and feel overwhelming, leaving shame or regret afterward. Others may feel unsafe near it.", "Shouting, slamming doors, threats, or sudden outbursts over small problems.", "Anger is a normal feeling. Repeated outbursts that frighten people are a pattern to address with help and practice.", ""],
  ["RAPID BREATHING / TACHYPNEA", "B", "Breathing that is faster than usual, often linked to fear, illness or hard exertion.", "Breaths come quickly and shallowly. The chest can feel tight and the head can feel light.", "Quick, shallow breaths, with the chest and shoulders rising and falling fast.", "Fast breathing during panic usually passes. Breathing that stays fast needs a medical check.", ""],
  ["RAPID EYE MOVEMENT SLEEP BEHAVIOR DISORDER", "D", "A sleep condition in which a person acts out vivid dreams, sometimes with shouting or sudden movement.", "Dreams can feel very real and intense, and the body may move before the person wakes. Injury is a real risk.", "Punching, kicking, yelling or talking in sleep, which can injure a bed partner.", "It is not a sign of violent intent. A sleep doctor can treat it, and it can sometimes be an early sign of other brain conditions.", ""],
  ["RAPID HEARTBEAT", "B", "A heartbeat faster than usual, which can feel alarming even when it is harmless.", "The heart may pound or flutter, and the chest may feel tight or uneasy. The sensation can come and go, and it can be worrying to notice.", "Hand on the chest, pausing to rest, or saying that the heart is racing.", "A fast heartbeat is often caused by stress, caffeine or exercise. Chest pain, fainting or a heartbeat that will not settle needs prompt medical care.", ""],
  ["RATIONALIZATION", "T", "Explaining away a feeling or action with reasons that sound sensible but hide the real cause.", "The explanation feels comfortable and brings quick relief. The deeper feeling may stay unexamined.", "Giving many reasons for a choice, or saying 'it was not that bad' after a hurtful moment.", "Everyone explains things to themselves sometimes. The habit becomes a problem when reasons keep the truth out of reach.", ""],
  ["RE-EXPERIENCING", "D", "Feeling a past trauma again through memories, flashbacks, nightmares or strong body reactions.", "The past can feel present, and the body may react before the mind understands why. Memories can arrive without warning and feel as sharp as the first time.", "Startling easily, sudden distress at reminders, or seeming lost in a memory.", "Re-experiencing is a symptom of trauma, not a sign of weakness. Therapy focused on trauma can ease it.", ""],
  ["REACTION FORMATION", "T", "Acting in the opposite way to a feeling that seems unacceptable to you.", "A strong feeling is pushed away and replaced by its opposite. This can feel safe but also strained.", "Excessive praise of someone you privately resent, or strong opposition to a wish you secretly hold.", "It is an automatic, protective habit that many people use. Noticing it helps, and honest feelings can be faced gradually.", ""],
  ["REACTIVE ABUSE", "R", "An informal term for an angry reaction to ongoing mistreatment, sometimes used to blame the hurt person.", "The person reacting may feel exhausted, afraid and pushed past their limit. Shame often follows the outburst.", "Sharp anger after repeated hurt, with the other person pointing to that anger as the main problem.", "A strong reaction under long mistreatment is human. It does not erase the harm, and it is not a clinical diagnosis.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help you plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["REACTIVE ATTACHMENT DISORDER", "D", "A rare condition in children who had serious neglect or unstable care, making trust in caregivers hard.", "A child may feel unsafe with caregivers, and closeness can feel frightening or confusing. The child may push away comfort even when it is offered.", "Avoiding comfort, not seeking help when hurt, or seeming guarded and hard to reach.", "It is not caused by a child's bad behavior. Consistent, caring relationships and specialist therapy can help children build trust.", ""],
  ["REALITY QUESTIONS", "K", "A practice of asking simple questions that check whether a thought matches the facts.", "Questions can create a little distance from a worry. They show what is known and what is only feared.", "Asking what evidence exists, what a trusted friend would say, or what is actually happening now.", "It is not arguing yourself out of feelings. It is a gentle check that leaves room for what is true.", ""],
  ["REASSURANCE SEEKING", "T", "Repeatedly asking others for comfort or certainty about a worry, even after you have been reassured.", "Reassurance brings relief for a short time, then doubt returns and asks for more. The need can feel urgent.", "Asking 'are you sure?' many times, checking messages again, or asking the same question in new ways.", "Wanting comfort is human. The pattern is when reassurance keeps the worry going instead of easing it.", ""],
  ["RECLAMATION", "U", "Taking back parts of yourself or your life that were lost or taken from you.", "Reclaiming can bring pride and a sense of ownership. It may also stir grief for what was lost.", "Speaking your own name, choosing your own path, or returning to activities you once loved.", "Reclaiming is not forgetting what happened. It is choosing what your life will include from here.", ""],
  ["RECURRENT ISOLATED SLEEP PARALYSIS", "B", "Repeated brief spells of being unable to move while falling asleep or waking, which can feel frightening.", "The body feels heavy and frozen while the mind is awake. Fear can be intense, and it usually passes within minutes.", "Waking with a startled look, or being unable to speak or move for a short time.", "It is usually not dangerous, though it can be frightening. A doctor should review it if it happens often or disrupts sleep.", ""],
  ["REDUPLICATIVE PARAMNESIA", "D", "A rare false belief that a familiar place has been copied, so that it exists in two locations.", "The belief can feel completely real, and the person may feel confused, uneasy or lost in familiar surroundings. Reassurance from others often does not change it.", "Insisting that a place is not the real one. Arguing that a familiar building has been moved or doubled.", "This is a symptom of a brain or mental health condition, not a choice or a lie. Medical care can help.", "Talk with a doctor soon. For support now, call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["REFERENTIAL", "T", "Seeing neutral events as personal messages or signs that are meant for you alone.", "Ordinary moments can feel loaded with meaning. A sense of being singled out can be unsettling.", "Reading hidden messages into songs, shows or strangers' remarks, and asking others what they meant.", "Noticing patterns is a normal human trait. When these meanings feel fixed or cause distress, a doctor or counselor can help.", ""],
  ["REFRAMING", "K", "Looking at a situation from another angle to find a fairer or more helpful meaning.", "A new angle can loosen a rigid thought and bring some relief. It can take effort when the feeling is strong.", "Writing a worry down, asking what is actually true, and noting a more balanced alternative.", "Reframing is not forced positivity. It looks for a realistic view, which can include hard truths.", ""],
  ["REFRESHED", "M", "Feeling renewed and clear-headed after rest, time away or a change of pace.", "Energy can feel lighter, and worries may seem smaller. The mind and body can feel ready to begin again.", "Relaxed posture, more smiles, and a willingness to start new tasks.", "Feeling refreshed does not last forever. It is a good moment to notice what helped and do it again.", ""],
  ["REGRESSION", "T", "Going back to an earlier, more childlike way of coping when stress feels too big.", "The person may feel small, needy or unable to manage. Adult tasks can suddenly feel out of reach.", "Sulking, clinging, tantrums, or needing constant help with things they usually handle.", "Regression is a common response to stress or illness. It is usually temporary and eases with patience and support.", ""],
  ["REJECTION SENSITIVE DYSPHORIA", "M", "Intense emotional pain from feeling rejected or criticized, even when the signal is small or unclear.", "A small comment can feel like a deep wound. Shame and anger can rise fast and last for hours.", "Withdrawing quickly after criticism, becoming defensive, or apologizing again and again.", "This is not overreacting by choice. The pain is real, and it can ease with support, practice and therapy.", ""],
  ["RELATIONAL REPAIR", "K", "Mending a relationship after a hurt, through honest talk and steady effort from both people.", "Repair can bring relief and hope, though it can also feel exposing and slow. Progress often comes in small steps, with setbacks along the way.", "Saying sorry clearly, listening without defending yourself, and following through on changes.", "Repair is not pretending nothing happened. It means naming the hurt and working on it together, with care.", ""],
  ["RELATIONAL SOVEREIGNTY", "U", "Keeping your own identity, choices and limits while staying connected to others.", "Standing in your own choices can feel steady and clear. It can also bring fear of losing the relationship.", "Making your own decisions, saying no when needed, and staying respectful of others.", "It is not isolation or pulling away from everyone. It is being whole in a relationship, not merged into it.", ""],
  ["RELATIONAL TRAUMA", "D", "Lasting harm from hurtful, unsafe or betraying relationships, especially early in life.", "Trust can feel risky, and the body may stay on guard even in safe moments. Self-worth may have taken a hit.", "Watching for signs of danger all the time, pulling away when closeness grows, or fearing abandonment.", "It is not a personal failing. Healing is possible, often through safe relationships and trauma-focused care.", ""],
  ["RELIEVED", "M", "A calm release of worry after something hard ends or turns out well.", "Shoulders may drop and breathing may slow. Relief can bring tears, laughter or a rush of energy.", "Sighing, smiling, or sitting down after a stressful moment passes.", "Relief after a real problem ends is healthy. Relief from avoiding a task can keep worry going, so notice which kind it is.", ""],
  ["RELIGIOUS", "R", "Having a strong faith or religious practice that guides your values, habits and community life.", "Faith can bring comfort, belonging and meaning. It can also bring guilt when practice feels hard to keep.", "Regular prayer or worship, using religious language, or making choices guided by faith.", "Faith is not a sign of weakness or illness. Trouble can start when beliefs are used to shame or control people.", ""],
  ["REM SLEEP", "B", "The sleep stage with fast eye movements and the most vivid dreaming, a normal part of rest.", "Dreams can feel vivid, strange or emotional, while the body stays very still. Waking up can feel groggy if sleep was broken.", "Quick eye movement under closed lids, and in some people, talking or limb movement during dreams.", "REM sleep is healthy and normal. It can be disrupted by poor sleep, alcohol, stress or certain medicines.", ""],
  ["REPLACED", "M", "Feeling that someone or something has taken your place, leaving you hurt and less valued.", "Being replaced can feel like rejection and loss. It may bring anger, sadness or fear of being forgotten.", "Pulling away, sulking, withdrawing from a group, or insisting on being needed.", "Feeling replaced is painful, but it is not proof that you are unimportant. It can be talked through with someone you trust.", ""],
  ["REPRESSED EMOTIONS", "M", "Feelings that are pushed out of awareness and kept hidden from yourself and from others.", "A sense of numbness or tension can appear, even though the feelings are still there. They may come out in other ways.", "Sudden anger over small things, unexplained tiredness, or trouble naming how you feel.", "Keeping some feelings private is normal. Feelings that are always hidden can add to stress, and naming them helps.", ""],
  ["REPRESSION", "T", "A protective habit of pushing painful thoughts or memories out of awareness.", "Pain can feel less sharp for a while, but it may return through dreams, moods or body tension. Feelings can surface later in unexpected ways, such as irritability.", "Changing the subject, forgetting painful details, or seeming unaffected by a serious event.", "Repression is a common way the mind protects itself. It is not a deliberate lie, and memories can return when the person feels safe.", ""],
  ["RESILIENCE ANCHORING", "K", "A practice of linking yourself to steady sources of strength, such as a place or a person.", "An anchor can bring calm when life feels stormy. It works best when it is practiced before it is needed.", "Keeping a photo nearby, repeating a grounding phrase, or returning to a familiar routine when stressed.", "An anchor is not a shield against all pain. It is a steady point to return to while the storm passes.", ""],
  ["RESILIENCE FATIGUE", "M", "Exhaustion from always having to cope, bounce back and stay strong.", "The drive to keep going can wear thin. Hope may fade, and small problems can feel too big.", "Irritability, withdrawing from effort, or saying 'I am just tired of being strong'.", "Needing rest is not weakness. Resilience works better with support, breaks and help, not only more willpower.", ""],
  ["RESILIENT", "U", "Able to recover from setbacks and keep moving toward what matters to you.", "Resilience can feel steady, with a sense of having been through hard things and still standing. It often grows slowly, through many ordinary days of coping.", "Adjusting to change, asking for help when needed, and returning to routines after setbacks.", "Resilience is not toughness without feeling. It includes pain, rest and support from others.", ""],
  ["RESILIENT ADAPTABILITY", "U", "Adjusting well to change, uncertainty and new demands in daily life.", "Change can feel uncomfortable at first, then manageable. Trust that you can adapt slowly grows.", "Trying new routines, accepting new roles, and staying calm when plans shift.", "Adaptability is not always smooth. It can still take time and effort, and it does not mean ignoring what is hard.", ""],
  ["RESILIENT ADAPTATION", "U", "Adapting to hardship by changing how you live while keeping your core values.", "Adjustment can feel slow. Each small change can bring a little more steadiness.", "Finding new ways to manage work, home or health after a loss or a major change.", "Adapting is not giving up on who you are. It is finding workable ways to live well in new conditions.", ""],
  ["RESILIENT HEART WISDOM", "U", "Knowing from experience what your heart needs in order to stay strong and open.", "The heart can feel calm and steady, even after hard times. Choices may come with more inner clarity.", "Taking time to rest, reaching out to others, and making decisions based on care for yourself.", "Heart wisdom is not an excuse to avoid hard facts. It works best alongside honest thinking and support.", ""],
  ["RESILIENT INNER STRENGTH", "U", "A steady strength within you that helps you keep going through hardship.", "Inner strength can feel quiet and grounding. It can return even after a hard season.", "Keeping routines during stress, speaking up for yourself, and continuing despite fear.", "Inner strength does not mean handling everything alone. Asking for support is part of it.", ""],
  ["RESILIENT RADIANCE", "U", "A warm, bright quality that remains after pain and hardship have passed through your life.", "A sense of light and warmth can return slowly, bringing hope into daily life. It may come back in small moments, even after a hard season.", "Kindness and humor during tough times, and encouraging others while still struggling.", "Radiance does not mean always looking cheerful. It can sit alongside sadness, fear and tears.", ""],
  ["RESOURCEFUL", "U", "Good at finding ways to solve problems and using the help and tools around you.", "Being resourceful can bring a sense of control and confidence. Problems may feel more solvable.", "Asking for help, trying different options, and using what is already at hand.", "Resourcefulness is not doing everything alone. Using other people's help is part of it.", ""],
  ["RESTLESSNESS", "A", "A tense urge to move, fidget or change activity, with trouble settling down.", "The body may feel on edge, and sitting still can feel uncomfortable. The mind can feel impatient or uneasy.", "Pacing, fidgeting, shifting positions, or constantly checking a phone.", "Restlessness is common with stress, worry or lost sleep. Mention it to a doctor if it lasts, since it can have medical causes.", ""],
  ["RESTORATIVE REST", "K", "Rest that truly refreshes body and mind, rather than simply filling time.", "Good rest can bring a softer, calmer feeling and a little more energy. It can come in short breaks as well as sleep.", "Taking real breaks, putting screens away, and sleeping at steady times.", "Rest is not laziness. It is part of staying healthy and able to cope with stress.", ""],
  ["RETIFISM", "F", "A sexual interest in feet or shoes.", "The interest can feel private, strong and sometimes embarrassing. It may bring shame or confusion.", "Usually private, shown in fantasies or private searches.", "An interest is not the same as acting on it. Any act without clear, ongoing consent causes harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["REVENGE BEDTIME", "V", "Staying up late to claim personal time after a busy day, even when it costs needed sleep.", "Late hours can feel like the only free time. Guilt about being tired the next day often follows.", "Scrolling or watching shows after bedtime, then feeling tired and irritable in the morning.", "Wanting free time is healthy. The habit becomes harmful when it steadily takes away sleep and health.", ""],
  ["REVERENT SURRENDER", "K", "A calm letting go of what you cannot control, held with respect and trust.", "Letting go can bring relief and a softer, more open feeling. It may still stir fear at first.", "Pausing before reacting, accepting an outcome with grace, and not fighting every change.", "Surrender does not mean giving up on effort. It means putting down the struggle against what cannot be changed.", ""],
  ["REVERSE PSYCHOLOGY", "C", "Pushing someone to do what you want by telling them to do the opposite.", "The target may feel nudged, confused or resentful. Trust can drop once they notice the trick.", "Saying 'I bet you cannot do it' to get someone to try. Saying 'do not come' when you want them there.", "Not every playful suggestion is manipulation. It becomes a tactic when it steers someone against their own judgment.", ""],
  ["ROOTED EARTH CONNECTION", "U", "A feeling of being grounded and connected to the land, the seasons and the natural world.", "Time outdoors can feel steadying. Worries may soften, and the body may relax.", "Walking on grass, tending plants, or spending quiet time in natural places.", "Connection to nature does not require a farm or wilderness. Small moments outdoors can build the same feeling.", ""],
  ["ROOTED WHOLENESS", "U", "A grounded sense of being complete, with your past, present and values held together.", "Life can feel steadier when every part of you feels welcome. Inner conflict may ease.", "Speaking honestly, keeping promises, and living in line with your values.", "Wholeness does not mean having no conflict or pain. It means holding them without losing yourself.", ""],
  ["RUINOPHILE", "F", "A fascination with ruins, abandoned places and the traces of the past.", "Ruins can feel moving, mysterious or peaceful. They may bring a pull to explore and reflect.", "Visiting old sites, photographing abandoned buildings, or reading about lost places.", "Liking ruins is a common and healthy interest. It becomes a concern only if it leads to entering unsafe buildings.", ""],
  ["RUMINATING", "T", "Going over the same worries or hurts again and again without reaching a resolution.", "The mind keeps circling one thought, which can feel heavy and tiring. It can be hard to focus on anything else.", "Replaying conversations, talking about the same problem often, or seeming distracted.", "Thinking about a problem is useful at first. Ruminating is when the thinking loops without leading to action or relief.", ""],
  ["RUMINATION", "T", "Repetitive, looping thoughts about the past or future that are hard to stop.", "The thoughts can feel urgent, like a problem that must be solved, yet they keep returning. Sleep and focus often suffer.", "Replaying events, asking 'what if' many times, or being unusually quiet while the mind works.", "Rumination is common under stress and in low mood. It is a habit that can be changed, not a sign of being broken.", ""],
  ["RUMINATION DISORDER", "D", "A feeding condition in which food repeatedly comes back up from the stomach into the mouth.", "Food may return with little warning and little effort. The person may feel embarrassed, worried or ashamed.", "Food coming back up after meals, discomfort after eating, or avoiding meals in company.", "It is a medical condition, not a lack of willpower or a personal choice. It can be treated with medical and nutrition care.", "A doctor or eating-disorder specialist can help. In the US, call or text 988 any time you are struggling."],
  ["RUMOR MONGERING", "C", "Spreading unverified stories about someone to damage their reputation or win favor.", "The target may feel humiliated, isolated and unable to set the record straight. Trust in the group can break down.", "Whispering, sharing gossip as fact, or repeating claims without checking them first.", "Sharing news is not always rumor. The pattern is harmful when stories are spread to hurt someone or gain status.", ""],
  ["SACRED BOUNDARIES", "K", "Limits you hold as deeply important, protecting your time, energy and values.", "Holding a boundary can feel uncomfortable at first, then steadying. It can bring a sense of self-respect.", "Saying no clearly, leaving a situation that harms you, or ending calls that drain you.", "A boundary is not a punishment for others. It is a clear limit about what you will do, and it can be kind and firm.", ""],
  ["SACRED CREATIVE FLOW", "U", "Creating with full attention, feeling that the work matters beyond yourself.", "Time may slip away, and the work can feel like an offering. Doubt and self-judgment may quiet down.", "Long, absorbed sessions of drawing, writing, music or building, often with little interest in distraction.", "Flow is not something you can force. It often comes after steady practice and will not last every day.", ""],
  ["SACRED DAILY GRATITUDE", "K", "A daily practice of noticing and naming what you are thankful for.", "Gratitude can soften worry and bring a warmer view of ordinary life. It may feel awkward at first.", "Writing a few lines each day, or naming good moments at dinner.", "Gratitude does not mean ignoring pain or pretending everything is fine. It can sit alongside grief and anger.", ""],
  ["SACRED DAILY RITUAL", "K", "A simple, repeated daily routine that gives your day steadiness and meaning.", "A ritual can feel grounding and comforting, especially on hard days. It can make the day feel more intentional.", "The same morning tea, a short reading, or a calm closing routine at night.", "A ritual does not need to be elaborate or religious. Small, repeated acts can be enough.", ""],
  ["SACRED GRIEF PROCESSING", "K", "A respectful way of working through grief, honoring the loss and the feelings it brings.", "Grief can come in waves, sometimes tender and sometimes raw. Honoring it can bring some relief over time.", "Visiting a grave or memorial, sharing stories of the person, or setting aside time to remember.", "Grief has no set timeline. Processing it is not about getting over the loss but carrying it with care.", ""],
  ["SACRED REST", "K", "Rest held as a meaningful, protected part of life, not a reward earned through productivity.", "Rest can feel calming and fair, even when work is waiting. Guilt may show up first and then ease.", "Taking a planned day off, turning off notifications, or resting without apologizing.", "Sacred rest is not avoiding duties. It supports energy, health and clear thinking.", ""],
  ["SACRED STILLNESS", "K", "Quiet, unhurried time with no task, letting the mind and body settle.", "Stillness can bring calm and space. Restless thoughts may rise first, then gradually soften.", "Sitting quietly, breathing slowly, or watching the light change without a phone.", "Stillness does not mean emptying the mind. Thoughts can pass through while you keep returning to quiet.", ""],
  ["SADISTIC", "R", "Taking pleasure in causing or watching other people's pain, fear or humiliation.", "The person may feel power, excitement or relief when others suffer. Empathy may feel weak or absent.", "Deliberate cruelty, enjoying someone's distress, or repeatedly pushing others into fear.", "A moment of anger or cruelty is not the same as a sadistic pattern. The pattern is serious, and people need protection from it.", ""],
  ["SEXUAL SADISM", "F", "A sexual interest in causing or watching another person's pain or fear.", "The interest can feel intense and confusing. It may bring shame or fear about what it means.", "Usually private, shown in fantasies or private searches.", "An interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting on it without consent causes serious harm to real people.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["SAFE BELONGING", "U", "Feeling accepted and secure in a group or place where you can be yourself.", "Belonging can bring relief, warmth and the sense that you do not have to hide. Feeling safe with others can grow slowly, one honest conversation at a time.", "Easy laughter, honest talk, and people who include you without pressure.", "Belonging does not mean agreeing with everyone. It means feeling respected even when views differ.", ""],
  ["SAFE PLACE VISUALIZATION", "K", "Picturing a calm, secure place in detail to help settle the body and mind.", "The picture can bring a sense of safety and slower breathing. It may feel strange at first, then more natural.", "A person sitting still with eyes closed, breathing slowly, and a calm face.", "A safe place is not an escape from real problems. It is a way to steady yourself so you can face them.", ""],
  ["SAFE TOUCH RECLAMATION", "K", "Slowly rebuilding comfort with safe, chosen physical contact after harm or fear.", "Touch may bring tension, fear or relief. The body may need time to learn that it is safe.", "Choosing hugs or handshakes carefully, or stepping back when a touch feels unsafe.", "Needing distance from touch is valid. Reclaiming comfort happens at a pace you choose, never under pressure.", ""],
  ["SAFETY RESOURCING", "K", "Building up a supply of people, places and habits that help you feel safe.", "Having sources of safety can bring calm and a sense of backup. It makes hard moments feel less lonely.", "Keeping a list of trusted contacts, using calming routines, or visiting a familiar safe place.", "Safety resourcing is not avoiding all danger. It means preparing supports so you do not face stress alone.", ""],
  ["SARCASTIC UNDERMINING", "C", "Using sarcasm and mocking jokes to belittle someone while keeping room to deny it.", "The target may feel foolish, angry or unsure whether they are allowed to object. Confidence can wear down.", "Sarcastic comments in front of others, then 'I was only joking' when challenged.", "Joking is not always harmful. The pattern is harmful when repeated sarcasm wears someone down while the speaker denies it.", ""],
  ["SAVIOR COMPLEX", "R", "A strong urge to rescue others, often to feel needed or to stay in control.", "Helping can feel meaningful and important. Stepping back can feel frightening or useless.", "Taking over other people's problems, giving advice nobody asked for, or feeling hurt when help is refused.", "Helping people is a good thing. The pattern becomes unhealthy when it ignores others' choices and leads to burnout.", ""],
  ["SCAPEGOAT COMPLEX", "T", "A belief that you are always the one blamed, even when others share the fault.", "The belief can bring hurt, anger and a sense of being singled out. It can make trust feel unsafe.", "Frequent talk about being blamed, expecting criticism, or defending yourself before anyone speaks.", "Feeling blamed is painful and can be real. The belief is worth testing against examples and a trusted friend's view.", ""],
  ["SCAPEGOATING", "C", "Blaming one person or group for problems that have many causes, to deflect attention or gain support.", "The chosen person may feel hurt, isolated and unfairly punished. The group may feel relief while the real problem stays.", "Repeatedly blaming one person at work or at home. Pointing at a group as the cause of trouble.", "Groups sometimes do make mistakes that need accountability. Scapegoating uses blame to avoid honesty and fairness.", ""],
  ["SCHIZOAFFECTIVE DISORDER", "D", "A mental health condition with psychosis, such as unusual beliefs, together with major mood episodes.", "Thoughts may feel confusing or frightening, and mood can shift strongly, along with sleep and energy changes. Some people feel steadier once treatment and routine are in place.", "Unusual beliefs, talking about things others cannot see, and sudden mood or energy changes.", "It is a real medical condition, not a character flaw or a choice. Medicine and therapy help many people.", "Talk with a doctor soon. For support now, call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["SCHIZOID", "R", "A pattern of little interest in close relationships and few outward signs of emotion.", "Being alone can feel comfortable, while closeness may feel draining or unnecessary. Emotions may be quiet or hard to read.", "Preferring solitude, having few friends, a flat expression, or limited reaction to praise or criticism.", "Preferring solitude is not illness by itself. The pattern matters when someone wants connection but feels unable to reach it.", ""],
  ["SCHIZOID PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A long-running pattern of keeping distance from others and showing little emotion.", "Closeness may feel uncomfortable, and the person may feel detached from life. Loneliness can still be present.", "Few close relationships, little interest in praise or criticism, and a reserved or flat manner.", "It is not the same as shyness or choosing to be alone. Therapy can help people who want more connection.", ""],
  ["SCHIZOPHRENIA", "D", "A serious mental illness with psychosis, such as false beliefs or hearing things others do not hear.", "Reality can feel confusing or unsafe. Thoughts may feel scrambled, and it can be hard to trust what seems real.", "Unusual beliefs, talking to someone who is not present, withdrawing, or struggling to organize daily tasks.", "It is a medical illness, not a moral failing or a sign of violence. Treatment, support and routine care help many people live well.", "Get medical help soon. For support now, call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["SCHIZOPHRENIC", "D", "An older adjective linked to schizophrenia, now often replaced by phrases such as 'person with schizophrenia'.", "The word can feel like a label that sums up the whole person. People who live with the illness often feel misunderstood.", "Using the word as an insult or a shortcut, which can hurt people who have the condition.", "A person is not their diagnosis. Schizophrenia is a medical condition, and the person living with it is more than the word.", "Get medical help soon. For support now, call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["SCHIZOPHRENIFORM DISORDER", "D", "A psychotic illness that looks like schizophrenia but usually lasts a shorter time.", "Thoughts can feel confusing or frightening, and trust in one's own mind may wobble. The episode can be distressing.", "Unusual beliefs, reports of things others do not notice, or sudden changes in behavior.", "It is a real illness that needs medical care, not a personal failing. Many people improve with treatment.", "Get medical help soon. For support now, call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["SCHIZOTYPAL", "R", "A pattern of unusual beliefs, unusual speech and discomfort in close relationships.", "Thoughts may feel strange or hard to explain, and social situations can feel uneasy. Being around people can take a lot of effort.", "Unusual ideas or speech, and visible discomfort in close relationships.", "Unusual thinking is not a sign of danger. It can be understood and supported with therapy and care.", ""],
  ["SCHIZOTYPAL PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A long-running pattern of odd beliefs, perceptions or speech, with strong discomfort in close relationships.", "Social settings may feel intensely awkward, and the person may feel misunderstood or on guard. Trust in others can come slowly, with much caution.", "Odd or unusual beliefs, speech that is hard to follow, and few close friends.", "It is in the same family of conditions as schizophrenia, but it is not the same illness. Therapy and social support can help.", ""],
  ["SCOPOPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of being looked at, watched or noticed by other people.", "Being seen can bring a rush of fear, a racing heart and a wish to hide or leave. The fear can make ordinary errands and gatherings feel exhausting.", "Avoiding eye contact, staying out of sight, or turning away from people in a room.", "It is a real and common fear, not a sign of weakness. Gradual support and therapy can make being seen feel less threatening.", ""],
  ["SEASONAL AFFECTIVE DISORDER", "D", "Depression that returns at about the same time each year, often in fall and winter.", "Mood can sink, energy can drop, and sleep may change. Days can feel gray and heavy.", "Sleeping longer, withdrawing from people, or seeming low and tired during the darker months.", "It is a real medical condition, not laziness or a lack of willpower. Light therapy, counseling and medicine can help.", ""]
];
